{
  "lesson_key": "a1-dialog-03",
  "notes": [
    {
      "note_key": "a1-dialog-03-note-1",
      "title": "Talking about what you’ll do with จะ",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "block_key": "b1",
          "block_type": "paragraph",
          "content": "In the dialogue, Narin and Mali both ask จะดื่มอะไร — \"What will you drink?\". จะ comes before a verb and shows what someone intends or is going to do. Here, จะดื่ม asks about what someone plans to drink."
        },
        {
          "block_key": "b2",
          "block_type": "formula",
          "content": "จะ + [verb] = future intention"
        },
        {
          "block_key": "b3",
          "block_type": "example_group",
          "heading": null,
          "content": null,
          "examples": [
            {
              "example_key": "e1",
              "thai_script": "จะไปด้วยกันครับ",
              "paiboon": "jà bpai dûai-gan kráp",
              "translation_en": "We'll go together."
            },
            {
              "example_key": "e2",
              "thai_script": "ฉันจะดื่มกาแฟค่ะ",
              "paiboon": "chǎn jà dʉ̀ʉm gaa-faae kâ",
              "translation_en": "I will drink coffee."
            },
            {
              "example_key": "e3",
              "thai_script": "คุณจะไปที่ไหนคะ",
              "paiboon": "kun jà bpai tîi-nǎi ká",
              "translation_en": "Where will you go?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "concepts": [
        {
          "type": "pattern",
          "key": "ja_verb"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "note_key": "a1-dialog-03-note-2",
      "title": "Describing things: the noun comes first",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "block_key": "b1",
          "block_type": "paragraph",
          "content": "In the dialogue, Narin asks about กาแฟร้อน and กาแฟเย็น — hot coffee and iced coffee. ร้อน means \"hot\", while เย็น can describe a drink as cold or iced. Notice that the describing word comes after the drink in Thai."
        },
        {
          "block_key": "b2",
          "block_type": "formula",
          "content": "[noun] + [adjective] = descriptive phrase"
        },
        {
          "block_key": "b3",
          "block_type": "example_group",
          "heading": null,
          "content": null,
          "examples": [
            {
              "example_key": "e1",
              "thai_script": "กาแฟร้อน",
              "paiboon": "gaa-faae rɔ́ɔn",
              "translation_en": "hot coffee"
            },
            {
              "example_key": "e2",
              "thai_script": "กาแฟเย็น",
              "paiboon": "gaa-faae yen",
              "translation_en": "iced coffee"
            },
            {
              "example_key": "e3",
              "thai_script": "ชาร้อน",
              "paiboon": "chaa rɔ́ɔn",
              "translation_en": "hot tea"
            },
            {
              "example_key": "e4",
              "thai_script": "ชาเย็น",
              "paiboon": "chaa yen",
              "translation_en": "iced tea"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "block_key": "b4",
          "block_type": "usage_tip",
          "content": "English usually puts the description before the noun, as in \"hot coffee\". In this Thai pattern, the order is reversed: say กาแฟร้อน, with the description after the noun."
        }
      ],
      "concepts": [
        {
          "type": "vocabulary",
          "key": "hot"
        },
        {
          "type": "vocabulary",
          "key": "cool"
        },
        {
          "type": "grammar",
          "key": "adjective_after_noun"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}